    "body": "Thanks @chipbuster - i guess you are right!\r\n\r\nIn fact i had to fix the same issue with git by setting `core.editor` to `io.neovim.vim`. I am a bit confused about the error since \"which\" gets it right.\r\n\r\nAnd in order to create a useful answer i flew over the sources of `which::which` as well. And it seems like the which implementation is not feature-complete. I guess it was created to get some kind of a cross-platform \"which\". But it also does this by searching the file tree for the given \"name\" which fails because vim/nvim isn't available under that name.\r\n\r\nI wonder why the which-based validation is done at all? (Edit: huh, this might be needed to have the full path for the subshell execution..)\r\n\r\nIn my opinion this case would be better handled by the shell completely, also letting the shell handle the error, wouldn't it? Who knows which fancy aliasing is being done by some magical shell?\r\n\r\nIf the validation is a must it should probably rely on a different mechanism like [`command -v vim`](https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/command.html) or the real `which`?\r\n\r\nAs for your request checking how other commands behave, they don't get it as well. `sudoedit`, `git commit` aren't picking it up aliases as well.\r\n\r\nAnd digging deeper i saw you invoking `/bin/sh`- without spending much time on thinking it through i guess you're spawning a subshell (using `sh`) to execute the commands (git commit and sudoedit may as well?). And my `sh`-environment isn't that properly configured yet. Some work's waiting there for me.. :D\r\n\r\nIf there's something i can do - please let me know.",
