    "body": "Okay. I'm at least relieved that the other programs don't get it either. I was having a hard time imagining how it could be possible without some serious sorcery.\r\n\r\nWithout getting too far into details, the problem is that once we launch starship, we lose a lot of the shell context, including your shell aliases. This is not unique to starship---if you try running the external `which` binary instead of using the shell builtin (e.g. by running `/bin/which nvim` on the command line), you'll see that it can't find `nvim` either, because it also doesn't know about your shell aliases. In essence, anything that isn't within the shell (like starship, git, sudoedit, etc.) cannot know about your shell aliases.\r\n\r\n> If the validation is a must it should probably rely on a different mechanism like [command -v vim](https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/command.html) or the real which?\r\n\r\nThis would be ideal, but when executing from within starship, we again don't have access to shell commands in the same context that you do. Even if we spawned a `zsh` instance to try to figure out what `nvim` is, it could fail if e.g. you've defined aliases in `.zshrc` instead of `.zshenv`. Also, we'd have to do this for the 6+ shells that we currently support, and that sounds like a nightmare.\r\n\r\n> Who knows which fancy aliasing is being done by some magical shell?\r\n\r\nYep, you've nailed the fundamental problem. As explained above, the problem is that once we execute an external program from the shell, we lose the ability to inspect the state of the shell (e.g. aliases) and there's no guarantee that launching a new shell will get that state back. As a really simple example, imagine that a user runs the following sequence in their shell to test some things:\r\n\r\n```\r\nexport EDITOR=myeditor\r\nalias myeditor=vim\r\ngit commit   # Launches EDITOR\r\nalias myeditor=io.neovim.nvim\r\ngit commit   # Tries to launch EDITOR\r\n```\r\n\r\nHow can the second `git commit` possibly know that myeditor is currently `io.neovim.nvim`? It can't read the shell startup files because that information isn't there (the alias was set at runtime). It can't (reliably) read the shell history files, because there might be additional commands in there that aren't relevant (e.g. if the user has elected to share shell history across all sessions). The only way would be if it could send its parent shell a DM asking what io.neovim.nvim is---unfortunately, shells simply don't support this.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
